  <section class="academy-lesson-extra" aria-label="Perl beginner lesson">
<h2>Perl essentials</h2>
<p>Perl is a flexible programming language used for text processing, automation, system administration and web applications. Perl programs can be short scripts or larger applications.</p>
<h3>Run your first program</h3>
<pre><code>perl -v
perl -e 'print "Hello, Academy!\n";'
perl hello.pl
perl -c hello.pl</code></pre>
<p><code>perl -v</code> shows the installed version. <code>-e</code> runs a one-line program, and <code>-c</code> checks syntax without running the program.</p>
<h3>Variables and decisions</h3>
<pre><code>use strict;
use warnings;
my $name = "Student";    # scalar
my @topics = ("Perl", "CPAN");
my %scores = (Perl =&gt; 10);
print "Hello, $name!\n";
if ($scores{Perl} &gt;= 10) {
    print "Great work!\n";
}
for my $topic (@topics) {
    print "$topic\n";
}</code></pre>
<p><code>$</code> names a scalar value, <code>@</code> an array, and <code>%</code> a hash. <code>my</code> declares a lexical variable. Start new scripts with <code>use strict;</code> and <code>use warnings;</code>.</p>
<h3>Subroutines and files</h3>
<pre><code>sub greet {
    my ($who) = @_;
    return "Welcome, $who";
}
print greet("Academy"), "\n";

open my $fh, '&lt;', 'notes.txt' or die "Cannot open: $!";
while (my $line = &lt;$fh&gt;) {
    print $line;
}
close $fh;</code></pre>
<p>Subroutines group reusable logic. <code>open</code> reads or writes files; always handle errors. To explore Perl's built-in documentation, try <code>perldoc perlintro</code> and <code>perldoc perlfunc</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Create <code>hello.pl</code>, print your name and three favorite topics, then run <code>perl -c hello.pl</code> followed by <code>perl hello.pl</code>.</p>
</section>
